  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: An upper level ridge exiting the region\nwill leave zonal flow aloft, keeping the weather relatively calm \nthrough early next week. Temperatures through Monday will remain \nabove normal for this time of year, with highs remaining in the \nupper 60s/lower 70s. Low temperatures will start in the low 30s \ntonight, and will continue to rise to the upper 30s/low 40s early \nnext week. \n\nIf you're looking for a chance to go outside and enjoy the\noutdoors, the best chances for this are this weekend, as looking\ninto next week we start to see influences of the polar jet.\nStarting Tuesday, the base of an upper level trough will extend\ninto southern California, bringing plenty of pacific moisture and\nlift to our region. This will bring chances for precipitation for\nthe remainder of the forecast period, with the highest chances on\nThursday. Additionally, there is a low chance of isolated\nthunderstorms on Tuesday. In terms of precipitation types,\ntemperatures remain high keeping it mostly rain, however some\nmountain peaks will see the potential for some overnight snow. As\na result of precipitation and increased cloud cover, high\ntemperatures will drop to the upper 50s and low 60s, while low\ntemperatures will drop to the mid 30s. Models continue to push\nback the timing of a cold front into late next week, continuing to\ngive low confidence on timing and intensity at this time. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in the forecast\nthrough Monday. Medium confidence in the precipitation and\ntemperature change next week. Low confidence on the chances of a\ncold front late next week.",
